\begin_layout Standard
CHAPTER 2 RIGHTS AND FREEDOMS 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part 1:
 Fundamental Rights and Freedoms Division 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1:
 The Bill o f Rights 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Right to life 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.1 Everyone’s right to life shall be protected by law.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.2 No one shall be deprived of life intentionally.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.3 Deprivation of life shall not be regarded as inflicted in contravention of this Article when it results from the use of force which is no more than absolutely necessary —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 in defence of any person from unlawful violence;
 or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 in action lawfully taken for the purpose of quelling a riot or insurrection.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.4 No one shall be condemned to death or executed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Freedom from torture 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
No one shall be subjected to torture or to cruel,
 inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.
 Freedom from slavery and forced labour 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.1 No one shall be held in slavery or servitude.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.2 No one shall be required to perform forced or compulsory labour.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.3 For the purpose of this Article,
 the expression “forced or compulsory labour” does not include —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 any work required to be done in the ordinary course of detention according to Article 5 or during conditional 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.
 Right to liberty and security 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 Everyone has the right to liberty and security of person.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 No one shall be deprived of their liberty except,
 on reasonable grounds and in accordance with fair procedures established by law,
 in the following cases —
 .
 1 the lawful detention of a person after conviction by a competent court;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 the lawful arrest or detention of a person for noncompliance with the lawful order of a court or in order to secure the fulfilment of any obligation prescribed by law;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 the lawful arrest or detention of a person effected for the purpose of bringing them before the competent legal authority on reasonable suspicion of having committed an offence or when it is reasonably considered necessary to prevent their committing an offence or fleeing after having done so;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.4 the lawful detention of persons for the prevention of the spreading of infectious diseases constituting a serious threat to public health,
 or of persons suffering from mental disorder where necessary for the prevention of harm to themselves or others;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.5 the lawful arrest or detention of a person to prevent their effecting an unauthorised entry into the United Kingdom or of a person against whom action is being taken with a view to deportation or extradition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.2 Anyone who is arrested shall,
 at the time of arrest,
 be informed in a language which they understand of the reasons for their arrest and shall be promptly informed of any charges against them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.3 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 It shall not be the general rule that persons awaiting trial shall be detained in custody.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 Anyone arrested or detained on a criminal charge shall be brought promptly before a judge or other officer authorised by law to exercise judicial power and is entitled to trial within a reasonable time or to release pending trial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 Release may be subject to guarantees to appear for trial or at any other stage of the judicial proceedings.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.4 Anyone who is deprived of liberty by arrest or detention is entitled to take proceedings before a court in order that the court may decide without delay on the lawfulness of the detention and may order their release if the detention is not lawful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.5 Anyone who has been the victim of unlawful arrest or detention has an enforceable right to compensation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.6 All persons deprived of their liberty shall be treated with humanity and with respect for the inherent dignity of the human person.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.7 Accused persons in detention shall,
 save in exceptional circumstances,
 be segregated from convicted persons and shall be subject to separate treatment appropriate to their status as people who have not been convicted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.8 .1 Accused juvenile persons in detention shall be separated from adults and brought as speedily as possible for adjudication.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 Juvenile convicted persons shall be separated from adults and accorded treatment appropriate to their age and legal status.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.9 No one shall be imprisoned merely on the ground of inability to fulfil a contractual obligation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.
 Right to fair and public hearing 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.1 .1 In the determination of their civil rights and obligations or of any criminal charges against them,
 everyone is entitled to a fair and public hearing within a reasonable time by an independent and impartial tribunal established by law.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 Judgment shall be pronounced publicly but the press and public may be excluded from all or any part of the trial to the extent strictly necessary in the opinion of the court:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 in the interests of public order or national security in a democratic society;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 where the interests of juveniles or the protection of the private life of the parties so require;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 where publicity would prejudice the interests of justice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.2 Everyone charged with a criminal offence is presumed innocent until proved guilty according to law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.3 Everyone charged with a criminal offence has the following minimum rights:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 to be informed promptly in a language which they understand and in detail of the nature and cause of the accusation against them;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 to have adequate time and facilities for the preparation of their defence;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 to defend themselves in person or through legal assistance of their own choosing or,
 if they have not sufficient means to pay for legal assistance,
 to be given it free when the interests of justice so require;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.4 to examine or have examined witnesses against them and to obtain the attendance and examination of witnesses on their behalf under the same conditions as witnesses against them;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.5 to have the free assistance of an interpreter if they cannot understand or speak the language used in court;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.6 not to be compelled to testify against themselves or to confess guilt.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.4 When a person has,
 by a final decision,
 been convicted of a criminal offence and has suffered punishment as a result of such conviction,
 and it is subsequently shown that there has been a miscarriage of justice,
 that person shall be compensated according to law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.5 Everyone convicted of a crime has the right to have their conviction and sentence reviewed by a higher tribunal according to law.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.6 No one is liable to be tried or punished again for an offence for which they have already been finally convicted or acquitted in accordance with the law and penal procedure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
7.
 Retrospective offences prohibited 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
7 .1 .
 .1 No one shall be held guilty of any criminal offence on account of any act or omission which did not constitute a criminal offence under national or international law at the time when it was committed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 Nor shall a heavier penalty be imposed than the one that was applicable at the time the criminal offence was committed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
7.2 This Article does not prejudice the trial and punishment of any person for any act which constitutes the crime of genocide or a crime against humanity.
 8.
 Respect for private and family life 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
8.1 Everyone has the right to respect for their private and family life,
 their home and their correspondence.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
8.2 There shall be no interference with the exercise of this right except such as is in accordance with the law and is necessary in a democratic society —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 in the interests of national security or public safety;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 for the prevention of disorder or crime;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 for the protection of health or morals;
 or .4 for the protection of the rights and freedoms of others.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
9.
 Freedom of thought
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
9.1.
 .1 Everyone has the right to freedom of thought,
 conscience and religion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 This right includes freedom to change one’s religion or belief,
 and freedom,
 either alone or in community with others and in public or private,
 to manifest one’s religion or belief in worship,
 teaching,
 practice and observance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
9.2 Freedom to manifest one’s religion or belief is subject only to such limitations as are prescribed by law and are necessary in a democratic society —
 .1 in the interests of public safety;
 or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 for the preservation of public order;
 or .3 for the protection of health or morals,
 or .4 for the protection of the rights and freedoms of others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
9.3 .1 No law shall be made establishing any religion or imposing any religious observance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 No religious test shall be required as a qualification for any office of public trust under any government in the United Kingdom.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
10.
 Right to education
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
10.1 No person shall be denied the right to education.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
10.2 In the exercise of their functions in relation to education and teaching,
 public authorities shall respect the right of parents to ensure such education and teaching in conformity with their own religious and philosophical convictions,
 so far as is compatible with the provision of efficient instruction and training and the avoidance of unreasonable public expenditure.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
11.
 Freedom of expression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
11.1 Everyone has the right to hold opinions without interference.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
11.2 .1 Everyone has the right to freedom of expression.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 This right includes freedom to seek,
 receive and impart information and ideas of all kinds regardless of frontiers,
 either orally,
 in writing or in print,
 in the form of art,
 or through any other media of their choice.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
11.3 The exercise of this right carries with it special duties and responsibilities.
 It may therefore be subject to certain restrictions,
 but only such as are provided by law and are necessary in a democratic society —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 for respect of the rights or reputations of others;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 for the protection of national security or of public order or of public health or morals.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
11.4 A requirement by law that radio or television broadcasting,
 or cinema enterprises,
 must be licensed is not inconsistent with this Article.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
12.
 Freedom of assembly and association 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
12.1 Everyone has the right to freedom of peaceful assembly and to freedom of association with others,
 including the right to form and to join trade unions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
12.2 No restriction shall be placed on the exercise of these rights other than such as are prescribed by law and are necessary in a democratic society —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 in the interests of national security,
 public safety or the preservation of public order;
 or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 for the protection of public health or morals;
 or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 for the protection of the rights and freedoms of others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
12.3 This Article does not prevent the imposition of restrictions prescribed by law and necessary in a democratic society on the exercise of this right by members of the Armed Forces or of the police or by persons charged with the administration of the state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
13.
 Rights in respect of marriage 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
13.1 Everyone of marriageable age has the right both to marry and to found a family.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
13.2 No marriage shall be entered into without the free and full consent of the intending spouses.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
13.3 .1 Spouses have equality of rights and responsibilities as to marriage,
 during marriage,
 and at its dissolution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 In the case of dissolution,
 provision shall be made for the necessary protection of any children.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
13.4 Every child has the right to such measures of protection as are required by their status as a minor,
 on the part of their family,
 society and public authorities.
 14.
 Right to enjoyment of possessions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
14.1 .1 Every natural or legal person is entitled to the peaceful enjoyment of their possessions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 No one shall be deprived of their possessions except in the public interest and subject to the conditions provided for by law and to prompt,
 adequate and effective compensation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
14.2 This Article does not in any way impair the right to enforce such laws as may be necessary to control the use of property in accordance with the general interest or to secure the payment of taxes or other contributions or penalties.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
15.
 Right to participate in public life and service
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
15.
 Every adult citizen has the right and the opportunity,
 without unreasonable restrictions —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 to take part in the conduct of public affairs directly or through freely chosen representatives;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 to vote and to stand for election at genuine periodic elections,
 which shall be by universal and equal suffrage and shall be held by secret ballot,
 guaranteeing the free expression of the will of the people;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 to participate,
 on general terms of equality,
 in public service.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
16.
 Freedom of movement 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
16.1 Everyone lawfully within the United Kingdom has the right of liberty of movement and freedom to choose their residence within the United Kingdom.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
16.2 Everyone is free to leave the United Kingdom,
 and everyone holding British nationality is entitled to a passport.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
16.3 No restrictions shall be placed on the exercise of the rights set out in this Article other than such as are in accordance with law and are necessary in a democratic society —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 in the interests of national security,
 public safety or the preservation of public order;
 or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 for the prevention of crime or under an order imposed by a court on conviction of crime;
 or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 for the protection of health;
 or .4 for the protection of the rights and freedoms of others.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
16.4 The rights set out in paragraph
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 may also be subject,
 in particular areas,
 to restrictions imposed in accordance with law and justified by the public interest in a democratic society.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
17.
 Freedom from expulsion from United Kingdom 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
17.1 No British National shall be expelled from the United Kingdom or deprived of the right to enter the United Kingdom.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
17.2 .1 Other persons may be expelled from the United Kingdom only in pursuance of a decision reached in accordance with law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 Any such persons who have been lawfully admitted to the United Kingdom shall be allowed,
 prior to expulsion,-
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 to submit reasons against expulsion;
 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 to have their cases reviewed by,
 and be represented for the purpose before,
 the competent authority or a person or persons especially designated by the competent authority.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
17.3 This Article does not prevent the extradition of persons,
 through established legal procedures,
 for the purpose of standing trial for a criminal offence or serving a sentence lawfully imposed on them in another jurisdiction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
18.
 Right of asylum 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
18.1 Every person has the right to seek and be granted asylum in the United Kingdom in accordance with the law of the United Kingdom and international conventions,
 if they are being pursued for political offences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
18.2 In no case may an alien be deported or returned to a country,
 regardless of whether or not it is their country of origin,
 if in that country their right to life or personal freedom is in danger of being violated because of their race,
 nationality,
 religion,
 social status,
 or political opinions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
19.
 Equality 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
19.1 Everyone has the right to recognition as a person before the law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
19.2 All persons are entitled without any discrimination to the equal protection of the law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
19.3 The equal protection of the law and the enjoyment of the rights and freedoms set out in this Bill of Rights shall be secured without discrimination on any ground such as sex,
 race,
 colour,
 language,
 religion,
 political or other opinion,
 national or social origin,
 association with a national minority,
 property,
 birth,
 homosexuality,
 disability,
 age,
 or other status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Division 2:
 Application and interpretation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
20.
 Application of Bill of Rights 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
20.
 The Bill of Rights applies to any act or omission by or on behalf of any person or body in the performance of any public function,
 including an omission by Government to take appropriate steps to secure compliance with any provision of the Bill of Rights.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
21.
 Scope of exceptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
21.
 Where the protection of any right or freedom by the Bill of Rights is subject to any restriction or qualification,
 that restriction or qualification —
 .1 has no wider effect than is strictly necessary in the circumstances;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 shall not be applied for any purpose other than that fir which it has been prescribed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
22.
 Interpretation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
22.1 The Bill of Rights —

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 is intended to give effect in the United Kingdom to the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights and the European Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 shall be interpreted and applied accordingly,
 but without prejudice to any rights and freedoms protected by the Bill of Rights which are more extensive than those protected by the International Covenant or the European Convention.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
22.2 Judicial notice shall be taken of- .
 1 the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights and the European Convention on Human Rights;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 reports and expressions of views by the United Nations Human Rights Committee;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 reports of the European Commission of Human Rights;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.4 judgments and advisory opinions of the European Court of Human Rights.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
22.3 Any question as to the meaning or effect of the International Covenant or the European Convention shall be treated as a question of law and,
 in the case of the European Convention,
 shall be for determination as such in accordance with the principles laid down by,
 and any relevant decision of,
 the European Court of Human Rights.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
23.
 Rights under other agreements 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
23.
 Nothing in this Chapter shall be interpreted as limiting or derogating from any of the human rights or fundamental freedoms which may be enjoyed under any other agreement to which the United Kingdom is a party.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
24.
 Abuse of freedoms 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
24.
 Nothing in the Bill of Rights shall be interpreted as implying for any group or person a right to engage in any activity or perform any act aimed at the destruction of any of the rights and freedoms set out therein.
 Division 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3:
 Remedies 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
25.
 Remedies 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
25.
 Without prejudice to any right to apply for judicial review,
 any person whose rights or freedoms protected by the Bill of Rights have been infringed or are threatened with infringement may bring civil proceedings for damages,
 an injunction or any other relief authorised by Rules of Court.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.
 Human Rights Commission
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.1 There is established by this Constitution a Human Rights Commission —

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 the composition of which shall be determined by Act of Parliament;
 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 the members of which shall be appointed by the Minister of Justice on the recommendation of the Public Services Commission.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.2 The Commission has the duty to encourage an understanding and acceptance of,
 and compliance with,
 the fundamental rights and freedoms guaranteed by the Bill of Rights.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.3 The Commission —

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 may investigate any act or practice which may be inconsistent with the Bill of Rights,
 whether on its own initiative or following a complaint;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 has the appropriate powers to secure the attendance of witnesses and the production of documents.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.4 The Commission has the power —

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 to assist individual complainants in legal proceedings in relation to the Bill of Rights;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 to institute such legal proceedings,
 whether or not it has received a complaint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.5 The Commission may challenge the validity of any provision of an Act of Parliament or of an Assembly that,
 in its view,
 is inconsistent with or in contravention of the Bill of Rights,
 by initiating legal proceedings in the High Court,
 the Court of Session or the High Court for Northern Ireland,
 as the case requires.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.6 The Commission may intervene in any proceedings that involve human rights issues,
 where it considers it appropriate,
 and with the leave of the court hearing the proceedings.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.7 The Commission may examine legislation and proposed legislation for the purpose of ascertaining whether it is in consistent with any of the provisions of the Bill of Rights and shall report any such inconsistency to Parliament.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.8 The Commission shall report to Parliament on the action which,
 in its opinion,
 needs to be taken in order that there is compliance with the Bill of Rights or any relevant international instrument.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.9 The Commission may prepare and publicise guidelines for the avoidance of acts or practices inconsistent within the Bill of Rights.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
26.10 The Commission shall submit an annual report to Parliament.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part 2:
 Social and Economic Rights 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
27.
 Social and economic rights 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
27.1 In making provision for the social and economic welfare of the people of the United Kingdom,
 Parliament and the Assemblies shall be guided by the principles contained in the International Covenants and Charters to which the United Kingdom is signatory,
 and in particular by—

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 the right of workers to earn their living in an occupation freely entered upon;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 the right of everyone to an adequate standard of living,
 including adequate food,
 clothing and housing;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 the right of everyone to social security;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.4 the right of everyone to the enjoyment of the highest attainable standard of physical and mental health;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.5 the right of everyone to education;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.6 the right of workers to resort to collective action in the event of a conflict of interests,
 including the right to strike;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.7 the right of every worker to enjoy satisfactory health and safety conditions in their working environment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
27.2 Parliament and the Assemblies of Scotland and Northern Ireland shall —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 secure that the working of the legal system promotes justice on the basis of equality of access;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 in particular,
 provide by law for legal aid and services to ensure that no one is prevented from securing justice by reason of economic or other disabilities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
27.3 The provisions of this Article are not enforceable in any court.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part 3:
 Freedom of Information 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
28.
 Access to official information 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
28.1 There is a right of access by the public to the information held by any public authority performing functions with respect to the government of the United Kingdom,
 a nation or a region,
 or to local government.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
28.2 This right is subject only to such limitations as are prescribed by law and are necessary in a democratic society —

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 for the protection of national security;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 in the interests of law enforcement or the prevention and detection of crime;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3 for the protection of personal privacy,
 legal privilege or commercial processes or transactions;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.4 to enable a public service to perform its constitutional functions or a public authority,
 when acting in the capacity of regulator,
 contractor or employer,
 to perform its functions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
28.3 Act of Parliament shall —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
 1 prescribe the procedures to enable the right of access to official information to be readily exercised and enforced;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 make provision for the appointment,
 powers and procedures of a Parliamentary Commissioner for Information.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
28.4 The Commissioner —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 shall investigate refusals of access to official informa- tion and complaints of delay or obstruction in the granting of such access;
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 may require public authorities to give access to official information or to compensate for unwarranted delays in giving such access.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
29.
 Use of information by public authorities 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
29.1 Any person required by law to provide information to a public authority shall be informed of the purposes for which that information is required.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
29.2 Any information so provided shall be used for those purposes only.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
29.3 No such information shall be communicated to any other public authority unless —
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.1 that communication is authorised by law;
 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.2 at the time the information was required,
 the person from whom it was required was informed that it might be communicated to the other public authority and of the puiposes for which it might be used by that other public authority.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
29.4 Paragraph
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.3.2 does not apply where the communication of the information is sought for the purposes of investigating fraud or crime.
\end_layout

